WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:06.417
Your tests pass. Then a worker dies, and a job never comes back.

00:00:06.597 --> 00:00:10.680
Race Lab reproduces the failure and checks a Meta-generated repair.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:16.537
Built by Tang Vu during this hackathon, using earlier TEND source and
event infrastructure.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.633
This is a real TypeScript method from a pinned public repository.

00:00:24.813 --> 00:00:30.625
It runs against a real, disposable database, inside a separate worker
process.

00:00:30.805 --> 00:00:35.359
Claim the job. Commit the row. Stop the worker.

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:40.194
The database survives. The original method leaves the job claimed.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:45.558
Now start a replacement worker on the same database.

00:00:45.738 --> 00:00:50.915
The trace shows exactly what committed, and when the old process exited.

00:00:51.095 --> 00:00:53.250
The original misses the claimed row.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:58.765
The repair reclaims it after the declared sixty-second lease, without
changing its payload.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:11.146
Meta's model sees the source and the contract, then chooses a crash
probe.

00:01:11.326 --> 00:01:13.918
The tool returns an actual failure.

00:01:14.098 --> 00:01:19.384
Next, Meta writes a candidate method; independent code executes and
scores it.

00:01:19.564 --> 00:01:24.589
Open the hypothesis, feedback and diff. The model never awards itself a
pass.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.997
But recovering one job is only half the story.

00:01:35.177 --> 00:01:40.713
A worker may still be alive. Or it may return with an old token.

00:01:40.893 --> 00:01:46.701
The lifecycle adapter tests contention, interruption, renewal and stale
ownership.

00:01:46.881 --> 00:01:52.048
Both feedback candidates pass all ten conditions, with guards inside
their source.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:59.279
A good experiment needs controls, not just a green badge.

00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:04.305
Unchanged OpenMusic code passes fourteen bounded checks.

00:02:04.485 --> 00:02:11.746
The air rules diff function meets six of eight checks. It fails two
under our added line-edit contract.

00:02:11.926 --> 00:02:16.187
These are source adapters, not full application runs or human feedback.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.304
We also compared direct repair with repair using executed feedback.

00:02:21.484 --> 00:02:26.492
Across twelve attempts, feedback passes six of six; direct, four of six.

00:02:26.672 --> 00:02:30.153
Failures include truncation and an invented SQL column.

00:02:30.333 --> 00:02:39.171
Seven previously unrun attempts were collected later, under a separately
frozen continuation. This small study proves no general superiority.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:45.736
Inspect the evidence online. Recompute the source locally, without a
model key.

00:02:45.916 --> 00:02:51.244
Switch methods. Scrub the trace. Export a regression, then import it and
check again.

00:02:51.424 --> 00:02:56.093
RaceLab: don't trust the patch. Give it a test it can fail.
